  "body": "## What happens\n\nFound while working #3300. The 5-axis stamp (`crates/motion/src/multiaxis/stamp.rs`) gives the approach feed the lean of its end station. The approach feed starts upright at the clearance and ends at the first contact. So the rotaries turn during the descent to the stock.\n\nPosted on `assets/machines/tilthead.mch` with `assets/posts/fanuc.cps` (the #3300 case, 5-Axis Contour on a spherical cap, clearance 20 mm, G54 at Z 50):\n\n```text\nG43.4 H01\nG00 Z-30.\nG00 X0. Y-3.84 Z-30. A0. B0.\nG01 X-0.776 Y-3.149 Z-47.314 A13.313 B15.42 F100\n```\n\nThe G01 block turns A by 13.3 degrees and B by 15.4 degrees while the tool moves 17 mm down to the part. Flow5D and Swarf show the same opening (A21.6 B9.8, and A32.5 B17.9).\n\nThis is not new with #3300. On a machine with no stated rotary rate, main posts the same G01 block today. #3300 only makes the start angle known (A0 B0 at the clearance).\n\n## Expected\n\nThe rotaries go to the first lean at a safe height, then the tool approaches the part with a constant pose: retract, rotate, then approach. A rotary move near the stock is the exception, and a judgment states why it is safe.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `cam.verify()` passes this program. It is not proven that the turn gouges. This issue asks for a decision on the approach law.\n- A fix changes the stamp's pose law for the approach stations (a held lead-in pose, or a rapid that turns at the clearance). It changes posted G-code, so the safety floor applies.\n\nDone means: the opening approach of a leaning 5-axis path does not turn a rotary below the clearance, or a decision records why the current law is safe. A test pins the chosen law with the tilt-head machine.\n",
